## 4. Chain of Responsibility

The **Chain of Responsibility pattern** avoids coupling the sender of a request to its receiver by giving more than one object a chance to handle the request. Receivers are chained together, and the request passes along the chain until an object handles it or the chain terminates.

### Technical Invariants

1. **Implicit Receiver Selection:** The client submits a request to the head of the chain; it does not know or care which link in the chain will ultimately handle it.
2. **Successor Delegation:** Each link in the chain holds a reference to the `next` link and decides whether to process the request, pass it along, or halt the pipeline.

```java
// Request context
public record HttpRequestContext(String user, String token, String role, String payload) {}

// Handler abstraction
public abstract class HttpHandler {
    private HttpHandler next;

    public HttpHandler linkWith(HttpHandler next) {
        this.next = next;
        return next;
    }

    public void handle(HttpRequestContext ctx) {
        if (process(ctx) && next != null) {
            next.handle(ctx);
        }
    }

    // Return true to continue down the chain, false to short-circuit
    protected abstract boolean process(HttpRequestContext ctx);
}

// Concrete Link 1: Auth Token Check
public class AuthenticationHandler extends HttpHandler {
    @Override
    protected boolean process(HttpRequestContext ctx) {
        if (ctx.token() == null || ctx.token().isBlank()) {
            System.out.println("401 Unauthorized: Missing bearer token.");
            return false; // Short-circuit
        }
        return true;
    }
}

// Concrete Link 2: Role Authorization Check
public class RoleCheckHandler extends HttpHandler {
    @Override
    protected boolean process(HttpRequestContext ctx) {
        if (!"ADMIN".equals(ctx.role())) {
            System.out.println("403 Forbidden: Admin privileges required.");
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### Real-World Occurrences
- Java Servlet `FilterChain`.
- Express.js / Node.js middleware pipeline (`(req, res, next) => { ... }`).
- Spring Security filter chains.

---

## 5. State Pattern

The **State pattern** allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes. The object will appear to change its class.

Unlike an unwieldy state machine driven by giant `switch (state)` statements across every method, the State pattern encapsulates each state as an independent class implementing a state interface.

### Technical Invariants

1. **State Polymorphism:** State-specific logic is partitioned across subclasses. Adding a new state requires only adding a new state class without mutating existing ones.
2. **Context Delegation:** The Context holds a reference to the `CurrentState` object and delegates actions to it: `state.handleAction(context)`.

```java
// State interface
public interface OrderState {
    void pay(OrderContext ctx);
    void ship(OrderContext ctx);
    void cancel(OrderContext ctx);
}

// Context
public class OrderContext {
    private OrderState state = new PendingPaymentState();

    public void setState(OrderState state) { this.state = state; }

    public void pay() { state.pay(this); }
    public void ship() { state.ship(this); }
    public void cancel() { state.cancel(this); }
}

// Concrete State: Pending Payment
public class PendingPaymentState implements OrderState {
    @Override
    public void pay(OrderContext ctx) {
        System.out.println("Payment processed successfully.");
        ctx.setState(new PaidState());
    }

    @Override
    public void ship(OrderContext ctx) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot ship an unpaid order!");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(OrderContext ctx) {
        System.out.println("Order cancelled.");
        ctx.setState(new CancelledState());
    }
}

// Concrete State: Paid
public class PaidState implements OrderState {
    @Override
    public void pay(OrderContext ctx) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Order is already paid.");
    }

    @Override
    public void ship(OrderContext ctx) {
        System.out.println("Dispatching parcel via courier.");
        ctx.setState(new ShippedState());
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(OrderContext ctx) {
        System.out.println("Refunding money and cancelling order.");
        ctx.setState(new CancelledState());
    }
}
```

